      "math_academy_id": "e-1239",
      "problem": "Given that $x\\lt 0,$ simplify $|{-6x}|+2x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|6x|+2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6x|+2x & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |x|+2x & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |x|+2x & = \\\\ 6|x|+2x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x \\lt 0,$ we have that $|x|=-x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6|x|+2x & = \\\\ 6(-x)+2x & = \\\\ -6x+2x & = \\\\ -4x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
